  "result": "Not quite. A language model remembers nothing between conversations on its own, so your assistant won't remember last week's chat unless you build that in.\n\nHere's what happens:\n\n**Monday:** Sam says, \"I finished the laser cutter training, but I'm still waiting on the 3D printer one.\" The assistant replies. When the session ends, nothing is kept.\n\n**Next Monday:** Sam asks, \"Can I book the laser cutter?\" The model sees only the text it's handed right now, so it has no idea who Sam is or what they finished.\n\nTo fix that, your code has to do the remembering. It saves \"Sam: laser cutter ✅, 3D printer ⏳\" to a file or database. Next session it looks that up and pastes it into the text the model reads. The model only seems to remember because you re-supplied the information.\n\nThis matters for your design because deciding what to save between sessions is your job, and every saved line you paste back in costs tokens. You'll want to save short facts like training status, not whole chat logs.\n\nQuick check: in the Monday/next Monday example, which piece does the remembering, the model or your code?",
